--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/lm-head-precision.md
@@ -0,0 +1,81 @@
+# Optional LM-head compute precision
+
+`--lm-head-compute-dtype float32` widens only the final, unquantized
+hidden-to-vocabulary projection. The Python equivalent is:
+
+```python
+ServerArgs(model="/path/to/model", dtype="float16", lm_head_compute_dtype="float32")
+```
+
+Omit the option (`lm_head_compute_dtype=None` in Python) to retain existing
+native arithmetic, dtype and kernel selection. There is no implicit change
+for FP16 models. The only supported explicit value is `float32`; other
+values are rejected in both CLI and Python configuration.
+
+## Arithmetic and scope
+
+The override computes the projection as
+`F.linear(hidden_states.float(), weight.float(), bias.float())` (without a
+bias when none was supplied). Inputs, accumulation and output logits use
+FP32, with autocast disabled around this operation. This is **not** a cast
+of an already-rounded FP16/BF16 result. For example, scores `1` and
+`1 + 1/4096` both round to FP16 `1`; widening those rounded logits cannot
+recover the ordering, whereas projecting the stored half operands in FP32
+can retain it. The projection follows PyTorch's device/backend matmul
+policy; the existing TF32 launch controls remain separate.
+
+Stored parameters, checkpoint dtype, tied embedding weights and incoming
+activations are unchanged. No FP32 weight cache is kept: each forward sees
+the current weight. Transformer/recurrent layers, SSM state precision and
+cache precision are not changed by this option.
+
+The result continues through the existing `LogitsProcessor._get_logits`
+path: logit scaling, vocabulary gathering or layout exchanges, vocabulary
+padding removal, softcapping and then sampling. Both sampled rows and
+prompt-logprob rows use this projection. Distributed greedy argmax continues
+to select its already-prepared state by the emitted logits dtype.
+
+Supported heads have an ordinary dense `[vocab shard, hidden]` FP16, BF16 or
+FP32 weight, either without a quantization method or with the runtime's
+unquantized embedding/linear method. Hidden states must be 2D, use one of
+these dtypes, and match the weight's width and device. An explicitly
+supplied bias must match the vocabulary shard and device. Quantized methods
+(including stale methods that the native path might otherwise bypass), packed
+or unsupported weight dtypes, weightless quantized heads and incompatible
+shapes are refused rather than silently ignoring the override. Head checks
+run during communication preparation and also guard direct projection calls.
+
+The option is refused with a bitwise numerics envelope: this direct PyTorch
+projection does not use the envelope's planned batch-invariant GEMM.
+See [Numerics envelopes](design/numerics.md) for that contract and its
+explicit exclusion of topology invariance.
+
+## Cost and numerical limits
+
+For a lower-precision head, each projection converts its weight shard and
+selected hidden rows to FP32. A converted weight takes four bytes per element
+of **transient extra memory**, in addition to the unchanged stored parameter.
+Prompt-logprob chunking repeats that conversion per chunk. Wider logits take
+four bytes per element and increase vocabulary collective traffic relative
+to FP16/BF16 (typically twice as many payload bytes); BF16-only multicast
+logits gathering is not used for FP32 logits. Sharded-argmax configurations
+can still avoid the full-vocabulary gather. Conversion, GEMM and communication
+may all cost more time; measure on the intended device and workload.
+
+Higher final-head precision can prevent some low-precision rounding ties,
+but it does not eliminate differences in earlier activations, reduction
+order, kernels or parallel layouts. It is **not a topology-invariance
+guarantee**, a general greedy-agreement guarantee, or a claim that every
+model's TP1/TP2 divergence is fixed. Validate complete generations and
+logprobs on the intended deployment.
+
+## Focused CPU tests
+
+```sh
+python -m pytest -q test/runtime/test_lm_head_precision.py
+```
+
+These tests use real CPU tensors for native and widened projections, a
+half-precision rounding tie, tied-weight/input preservation, autocast,
+layout/gather results, configuration publication and rejection boundaries.
+Only the missing peer in the vocabulary-gather test is substituted.
